'' A TYPE NAME MEANS WHAT IT MEANT AT THAT POINT IN THE SOURCE.
''
'' A scope may declare a type with a name an ENCLOSING scope already uses, and a
'' reference written BEFORE that declaration means the OUTER one. The lookup was
'' innermost-first with no regard for position, so both overloads below took the INNER
'' T1 and collapsed to one name:
''
''     invalid redefinition of function '_ZN7NESTED19OVERLOAD1EPFvRNS_2T1EE'
''
'' Measured against fbc, which gives them the two names this asserts. The corpus file
'' this is reduced from -- tests/vendor/fbc-tests/pointers/procptr-namespaces.bas --
'' says so in its own comment: "it's a different T1 now, so the overloads are different
'' aswell".
''
'' THE VISIBLE HALF is the refused module. The quiet half is every declaration above
'' such a redeclaration silently taking the wrong type, which nothing would report.
''
'' ONLY WHEN AN OUTER SCOPE HAS ONE TOO: a forward reference with nothing else to bind
'' to keeps the later declaration, which is an ordinary incomplete type. And within ONE
'' FILE only -- across two, the include order decides and a raw comparison of positions
'' would be guessing.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-mangle tests/mangle/src/type-shadow-order.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0

'' R2T1E -- a reference to the GLOBAL T1, unqualified.
'' EXPECT-OUT: _ZN7NESTED19OVERLOAD1EPFvR2T1E

'' RNS_2T1EE -- a reference to nested1's own, through the substitution for the
'' enclosing namespace.
'' EXPECT-OUT: _ZN7NESTED19OVERLOAD1EPFvRNS_2T1EE
'' GOLDEN: expected/type-shadow-order.out
